cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  refractpath: slant-path atmospheric refraction with the cross-model
  disagreement measured and the validity floor computed
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-29
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Takes an observer location, an apparent elevation and an atmospheric state,
  and produces the refraction correction by five independent standard methods,
  their disagreement, and the elevation below which that disagreement exceeds a
  pointing tolerance the caller declares. The first output of every call is a
  computed validity verdict -- usable, degraded or refuse -- with its reasons
  named and five structural limits the library cannot determine from its inputs
  at any elevation. Baseline 1 is the closed forms that are actually used: the
  exact plane-parallel solution with its analytic floor at 90 minus arcsin of
  1/n, Oriani's two-term series with a breakdown elevation computed in closed
  form from the series coefficients, Bennett (1982) and Saemundsson (1986).
  Baseline 2 is a ray trace through a layered spherically symmetric
  refractivity profile, with refractivity from four independently published
  dispersion formulations. Validation is level 2. The validity floor at 1010
  hPa, 10 C, dry and 550 nm is 11.2448 degrees of apparent elevation for a 10
  arcsec tolerance and 69.789 degrees for 1 arcsec, measured by bisection to a
  bracket of 8.613e-05 degrees. The disagreement curve is measured to be
  non-monotone, with three tolerance crossings, and a search that assumed one
  crossing would understate the floor by a factor of 9.179. Five of six
  external published references are reproduced within their stated tolerances
  and one is not: Bennett's own published accuracy claim of 0.07 arcminutes is
  exceeded below 19.1361 degrees, with a maximum measured departure of 34.0737
  arcsec, and is published as measured. That claim was made against Garfinkel
  (1967), which this package does not implement, so the miss measures the
  difference between two reference atmospheres rather than an error in the
  published formula, and this package cannot attribute it. No transcription of
  a printed refraction table is shipped, because none was available to this
  build. Several results are unfavourable to the published closed forms and are
  published as measured: both navigational fits overstate the layered integral
  at every one of 54 swept states above 15 degrees, their measured usable range
  at a 10 arcsec budget is 15 and 30 degrees against a published range of 0 to
  90, and below 7 degrees the sign of their error is not constant across the
  state grid, so it cannot be removed by a scale factor. Below one degree the
  unmeasured temperature profile above the observer is the larger error term by
  factors of 6.9 to 8.8: three physically attainable surface lapse rates move
  the horizon refraction by 300.0181 arcsec and the 45-degree refraction by
  0.0014 arcsec. The ray trace was checked against a bracket derived from
  monotonicity and the mean value theorem, which shares no mathematics with the
  shell sum, and against the exact single-interface Snell solution in the
  flat-Earth limit, which it reproduces to 3.506e-06 relative. Nine controls
  were required to fire and nine fired; five were required not to fire and none
  did. Nothing here is compared against a measurement, and astropy, ephem and
  pyerfa are not installed in the measured environment. Research grade. Not
  flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
  No correction computed here is suitable for operational pointing below the
  measured validity floor.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - atmospheric-refraction
  - atmospheric-propagation
  - pointing
  - ray-tracing
  - refractivity
  - dispersion
  - astronomy
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: "Edlén"
        given-names: "B."
    title: "The refractive index of air"
    journal: "Metrologia"
    volume: 2
    issue: 2
    year: 1966
    notes: >-
      The dispersion of standard air, equation (5) of refractpath.refractivity.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Birch
        given-names: "K. P."
      - family-names: Downs
        given-names: "M. J."
    title: "An updated Edlén equation for the refractive index of air"
    journal: "Metrologia"
    volume: 30
    issue: 3
    year: 1993
    notes: >-
      Equations (6), (7) and (8). With the 1994 correction, Metrologia 31(4),
      315-316. The published value of 2.7653e-4 for standard air at 633 nm is
      the first of this product's six external reference checks.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Ciddor
        given-names: "P. E."
    title: >-
      Refractive index of air: new equations for the visible and near infrared
    journal: "Applied Optics"
    volume: 35
    issue: 9
    year: 1996
    notes: >-
      Equations (10) to (12), the only formulation here that computes air
      densities from an equation of state rather than an empirical pressure and
      temperature scaling. Its BIPM-1991 density of standard dry air is the
      second external reference check.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Bennett
        given-names: "G. G."
    title: >-
      The calculation of astronomical refraction in marine navigation
    journal: "Journal of Navigation"
    volume: 35
    issue: 2
    year: 1982
    notes: >-
      Equation (15), and the published accuracy claim of 0.07 arcmin that this
      product measures and reports as NOT reproduced below 19.1361 degrees
      against its own ray trace.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: "Sæmundsson"
        given-names: "Þ."
    title: "Atmospheric refraction"
    journal: "Sky and Telescope"
    volume: 72
    year: 1986
    notes: >-
      Equation (16), taken as given in Meeus (1998) chapter 16, which is the
      form this product implements.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Meeus
        given-names: "J."
    title: "Astronomical Algorithms"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Willmann-Bell"
    year: 1998
    notes: >-
      Chapter 16, Atmospheric Refraction. The source for equations (15), (16)
      and the pressure and temperature scaling of equation (17), and for
      Bennett's accuracy claim as quoted.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Smart
        given-names: "W. M."
    title: "Textbook on Spherical Astronomy"
    edition: "6th"
    publisher:
      name: "Cambridge University Press"
    year: 1977
    notes: >-
      Chapter 3, for Oriani's theorem, which is equation (14), for Bouguer's
      formula, and for the constant of refraction, the fourth external
      reference check.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Auer
        given-names: "L. H."
      - family-names: Standish
        given-names: "E. M."
    title: >-
      Astronomical refraction: computation for all zenith angles
    journal: "Astronomical Journal"
    volume: 119
    year: 2000
    notes: >-
      The refraction integral of equation (19) and the invariant of equation
      (18), in the form this product integrates.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Garfinkel
        given-names: "B."
    title: "Astronomical refraction in a polytropic atmosphere"
    journal: "Astronomical Journal"
    volume: 72
    year: 1967
    notes: >-
      NAMED, NOT IMPLEMENTED. This is the reference Bennett's accuracy claim
      was made against. Because it is not implemented here, the failure of that
      claim against this product's ray trace measures the difference between
      two reference atmospheres and is not evidence that Bennett's formula is
      wrong.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Buck
        given-names: "A. L."
    title: >-
      New equations for computing vapor pressure and enhancement factor
    journal: "Journal of Applied Meteorology"
    volume: 20
    year: 1981
    notes: >-
      Equation (4), the saturation vapour pressure over liquid water, compared
      in this product against the IAPWS form quoted by Ciddor (1996) and
      against the tabulated 2339 Pa at 20 C.
  - type: report
    title: "US Standard Atmosphere 1976"
    institution:
      name: "NOAA, NASA and USAF"
    year: 1976
    notes: >-
      The lapse rates and layer structure of equation (2), and the standard dry
      air density of 1.2250 kg/m^3 used as an external reference check. The
      profile is anchored on the observer's own measurements rather than on
      mean sea level, which is a modelling choice stated in the module
      docstring.
  - type: book
    editors:
      - family-names: Urban
        given-names: "S. E."
      - family-names: Seidelmann
        given-names: "P. K."
    title: >-
      Explanatory Supplement to the Astronomical Almanac
    edition: "3rd"
    publisher:
      name: "University Science Books"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      The low-precision refractivity form of equation (9), and the 34 arcmin
      horizon refraction adopted with 16 arcmin of semidiameter for rise and
      set, which this product records as a convention rather than a measured
      value.
